        {
            "category": "office",
            "content": "1. Completed invoice approve.\n2. Clarity and answer questions.",
            "created_at": "2026-09-04T04:16:56Z",
            "folder_id": "efd6e044-b2b9-4f8f-8c1a-6acc3f7bb37e",
            "id": "686eeb86-1564-4f8c-b709-9d8c4a80cc59",
            "title": "Today",
            "updated_at": "2026-09-04T04:16:56Z"
        },
        {
            "category": "office",
            "content": "$pdf = new Fpdi();\n\nCreates a brand-new, empty PDF document. This is the output we're building — it starts with zero pages. Fpdi is special: it's a normal PDF-writing class (like FPDF) that's also able to read pages out of existing PDF files and reuse them as content.\n\n$pageCount = $pdf->setSourceFile(StreamReader::createByString($pdfContent));\n\nFor each invoice's raw bytes: StreamReader::createByString($pdfContent) wraps the byte string in an in-memory reader (so FPDI can parse it without writing it to a temp file on disk first). $pdf->setSourceFile(...) parses that PDF and returns how many pages it has ($pageCount).\n\nimportPage($pageNumber) parses that one page's content and hands back an internal template ID. This doesn't add anything to the output yet — it's just \"here's page 3 of invoice A, ready to be reused.\"\n\n- getTemplateSize($templateId) reads that page's actual width/height/orientation. This matters because not every invoice PDF is guaranteed to be identical page size — this keeps each page in the merged file at its own original dimensions instead of stretching/cropping.\n\n- AddPage($orientation, [$width, $height]) starts a new blank page in the output document, sized to match.\n\n- useTemplate($templateId) stamps that imported page's content onto the new blank page, full-bleed.",
            "created_at": "2026-08-20T05:00:48Z",
            "folder_id": "90334a21-ac14-4a31-a797-3581b0a24773",
            "id": "aad9e2a8-c523-4c37-ae2b-7c4ea19aa5a8",
            "title": "PDF Merger",
            "updated_at": "2026-08-20T05:00:48Z"
        },
        {
            "category": "office",
            "content": "1. Use sprintf for string catenation.\n7. Every calculations, conversions and input passes should covers all possible internal error causes.\n8. Consider nullable value cases where there are nullable fields.\n2. Use private methods for only the important repeated processes.\n3. Use public setUp for repeated modelfactorytrait calls in test files.\n4. Follow AU standards for coding and reviews.\n5. Move major part of functions to a service instead of adding in controllers.\n6. Use abstract files (models, controller and others) for builds.",
            "created_at": "2026-08-25T04:59:22Z",
            "folder_id": "ff2b4a7f-321f-44f8-bad3-5bd579f45513",
            "id": "c6df11e9-eb3e-4f90-8c44-f6281c405274",
